import sys
p = sys.argv[1]
lines = open(p, encoding='utf-8').read().split('\n')
idx = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith('| 630 |')]
assert len(idx) == 1
lines.insert(idx[0] + 1, "| 631 | 2026-10-22 | **Keys and staff: her 14:31-14:41 feedback round.** (1) Counting 1-12 now draws a line from each number to its key, the current one in coral. (2) The right/wrong sentence now sits directly under the question in every lesson. (3) More try questions: Black keys 11 kinds, Naming the white keys 8, Every key has its place 8; the 'one step up/down' staff question marks the note's own key first. (4) Middle C is now the centre: the clef and middle-C keyboards span C3-C5, the staff-to-keys keyboard C2-C6, the names lesson C3-C5; every staff keyboard marks middle C with a 'C' label and a caption. (5) Treble and bass clefs: close-ups show middle C, G and F together; 'Both together' is anchored on middle C (notes C G / C F, keyboard split at middle C). (6) Letters lesson: the 'one place, one letter' step dropped; the lines/spaces sentences restored (Every Good Boy Deserves Fun, FACE, Great Big Dragon Flies Away, All Cows Eat Grass) at her request. (7) Five landmarks: replaced by a fold-at-middle-C picture (G and F mirror, the two Cs mirror) with the keyboard shown beside it. (8) Octaves: end-key numbers readable. **For her review:** the four mnemonic phrases are hers from the old version (restored on request); fold facts (G is 4 steps above, F 4 below; the Cs 7 steps) are from my own counting, check against her materials. |")
open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write('\n'.join(lines)); print('ok')
